# Security response headers for the deployed demo.
#
# Vite copies everything in `public/` into `dist/client`, and Cloudflare Workers
# static assets honor this `_headers` file, so these headers apply to every
# served STATIC ASSET.
#
# STATIC ASSETS ONLY - NOT `/api/*`. `wrangler.jsonc` sets
# `run_worker_first: ["/api/*"]`, which sends those requests straight to the
# Worker and bypasses the static-asset layer that applies this file. So none of
# the headers below reach a `/api/*` response, and generalizing "every served
# asset" to "every response" is wrong. `/api/*` gets its own, deliberately
# smaller set (a JSON response wants different headers than a document) from
# `secureHeaders()` in `src/worker/index.ts`. If your `/api` ever returns HTML or
# sets a cookie, that is the file to edit - not this one.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE TAKES EFFECT: the deployed Worker, and locally under
# `pnpm preview` (which runs `vite build` then `vite preview`). It does NOT take
# effect under `pnpm dev`. `@cloudflare/vite-plugin` feeds `_headers` to the dev
# server only when `experimental.headersAndRedirectsDevModeSupport` is enabled in
# `vite.config.ts`, and this demo leaves it off, so the dev server answers every
# request without these headers. Verify a change here with `pnpm preview`
# (or against the deployed demo), never with `pnpm dev`.
#
# This is a deliberately SAFE baseline. It does NOT set a resource-restricting
# Content-Security-Policy (`default-src`/`script-src`/`connect-src`/`frame-src`),
# because the Checktiv browser SDK loads a cross-origin capture iframe and calls
# the regional sdk-api, and a wrong policy would silently break those flows.
#
# To add a full resource CSP later, allow your Checktiv SDK region origins in the
# relevant directives and test the capture, reviewer, and cross-device flows
# against a real session before shipping it. The origins to allow are:
#   connect-src: https://sdk-api.us.checktiv.com https://sdk-api.eu.checktiv.com
#                https://workspace.us.checktiv.com https://workspace.eu.checktiv.com
#   frame-src:   https://embed.us.checktiv.com https://embed.eu.checktiv.com
#                (the capture origin, the same host the camera grant below names)
#                plus the workspace origins above
#
# This demo installs the SDK from npm, so its own origin serves the script and no
# `script-src` entry for the Checktiv CDN is needed. A script-tag install would
# also need https://sdk.us.checktiv.com / https://sdk.eu.checktiv.com there.
#
# CAMERA: why the `Permissions-Policy` below names the capture origin.
#
# The camera opens inside a cross-origin capture frame the SDK renders, and that
# frame is served from the CAPTURE origin, `embed.<region>.checktiv.com`. The SDK
# sets `allow="camera"` on the frame, which delegates the feature to the frame's
# own origin, so the capture origin is the one a `camera` allowlist must name.
#
# It is NOT this page's origin, and it is NOT the CDN origin
# (`sdk.<region>.checktiv.com`) that serves the SDK script on script-tag installs.
# The CDN origin is the intuitive wrong guess, because it is the host in a
# `<script>` tag and the host in stack traces; the browser matches a camera grant
# against the frame's own document origin, never against where its scripts came
# from. Naming it here leaves the capture frame with no camera grant at all.
#
# This entry is NOT required for the SDK to work. A page that declares no
# `camera` directive at all leaves the feature undeclared, the frame inherits it,
# and the camera opens; that is why an integration with no `Permissions-Policy`
# header works unchanged. It becomes required the moment a page DECLARES a
# `camera` allowlist, because a declared allowlist that omits the capture origin
# denies the frame the camera and the applicant cannot finish verification.
# `camera=()` and an allowlist naming some other Checktiv host both fail that
# way, and the failure looks like a device problem rather than a header problem.
# This demo declares a restrictive policy on purpose, so the correct pattern is
# visible here instead of left as an exercise.
#
# Both regions are listed because this demo reads its region at runtime from the
# key the visitor pastes (`ah_sk_us_...` or `ah_sk_eu_...`), so either capture
# origin can be the live one. A single-region integration lists only its own.
#
# `self` stays in the allowlist even though this page never opens the camera
# itself. A document can only delegate a permission it holds: the frame's
# `allow="camera"` attribute narrows the parent's grant, it can never grant a
# permission the parent lacks. Omit `self` and the parent holds no camera
# permission at all, so there is nothing to delegate, and the frame is denied
# even though its own origin is listed. If you point the demo at a
# non-production cell (`VITE_CHECKTIV_DEV_CELL*`, see `src/shared/dev-cell.ts`),
# that cell serves its capture frame from a different host, so add that host
# while you test:
#   camera=(self "https://embed.us.checktiv.com" "https://embed.dev.example.com")
#
# WHY `form-action` IS SAFE TO SET HERE WHEN THE RESOURCE DIRECTIVES ARE NOT.
#
# The CSP below is deliberately NON-resource: it restricts what can FRAME this
# page, what `<base>` can rewrite, and where a form can POST - none of which the
# SDK uses. `form-action 'self'` is what stops an injected form from posting its
# contents to an attacker's origin, and `base-uri 'self'` alone does not cover
# that. It costs nothing here because this demo submits over `fetch`, never by
# HTML form submission. A resource directive (`connect-src`, `frame-src`) is a
# different matter: get one wrong and the capture iframe silently fails, which is
# why they stay absent until tested against a real session.
#
# Full guide: https://docs.checktiv.com/developers/sdks/security-headers
#
# HSTS: THE LINE BELOW MAY BE INERT, AND IS ON THIS DEMO.
#
# If your zone has HSTS enabled in the Cloudflare dashboard, the ZONE setting
# wins and the value here never reaches the wire. Verified on
# sdk-demo.checktiv.com: this file asks for `max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains`
# and the response carries `max-age=15552000; includeSubDomains; preload`. Nothing
# is unsafe (the served policy is the stronger one - it adds `preload`), but it
# means editing this line alone would change nothing. Set the value you actually
# want in the dashboard, and check the wire with `curl -sI`, not the file. Every
# other directive in this block DOES survive to the wire byte for byte; HSTS is
# the one exception, and only because of the zone override.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self "https://embed.us.checktiv.com" "https://embed.eu.checktiv.com"), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
